{
  "schemaVersion": 4,
  "id": "t01-005",
  "track": "track-01",
  "title": "C# nullability nullable value types and nullable reference analysis",
  "status": "source-backed-draft-generation-enabled",
  "concepts": [
    "Null means no value for a nullable value type or no object reference for a reference type. A nullable value type T? is a distinct Nullable<T> value type; nullable reference type annotations do not create a new runtime type.",
    "Nullable reference types are a compile-time annotation and static-flow-analysis system. They produce warnings and contracts, not runtime null checks, and the annotation context can differ from the warning context.",
    "The compiler tracks an expression's null state through assignments, branches, patterns, and recognized attributes. The analysis is useful but not proof: reflection, external code, deserialization, arrays, default structs, and suppressed warnings can violate annotations.",
    "Use control flow and validation to establish non-null values. The null-forgiving operator suppresses a warning but changes no runtime value; it is an assertion by the programmer, not a check.",
    "Public APIs communicate nullability as part of their contract. Flow-analysis attributes let libraries express conditional postconditions and input contracts when ordinary annotations cannot describe the relationship."
  ],
